## 7. Reading
The two unused cores (0, 1) carry no work of any config; in the B run they are as elevated as the pinned cores, so the
elevation is not caused by B's capture process or by any B-specific step above (all B-specific steps are outside the window
or run on the same cores for every config). The sections list and the in-window command path are identical across configs
except the loader's 1 Hz map reads. This points to contamination by ambient load, not a parity violation; one run per config
cannot prove it, which is what the canary rule (V6') and idle windows (A2) are for.
